The human brain is incredibly efficient because it activates only the specific neurons needed for a thought. Modern large language models (LLMs) attempt to do this as well, with over 95% of neurons in feedforward layers remaining silent for any given word. However, hardware limitations often hinder this efficiency, as unstructured sparsity leads to irregular memory access, making computations slower on GPUs designed for dense operations.

To address this issue, a collaboration with @NVIDIA resulted in the development of a 'Hybrid' format called TwELL, which reshapes sparsity to fit GPU architecture. This format dynamically routes 99% of highly sparse tokens through a fast path while using a dense backup matrix for rare, heavier tokens. The implementation of TwELL and new custom CUDA kernels for LLM inference and training achieved over 20% faster training and inference on H100 GPUs, alongside reductions in energy consumption and memory requirements.

@SakanaAILabs: "How do we make LLMs faster and lighter? Don’t force the GPU to adapt to sparsity. Reshape the sparsity to fit the GPU!"

@mmbronstein, your contribution is very visible, Michael! Have a look at the plot I made for the EU. I ran an analysis of EU-affiliated papers at ICLR 2026. The picture is less negative than the original treemap may suggest, but still, Europe needs to wake up.

1,043 out of 5,355 papers have at least one EU affiliation, which is a 19.48% incidence on all accepted papers. If we count each paper once for every EU country represented among its authors, the total rises to 1,211 paper-country incidences. Germany leads by a large margin, followed by France and Italy. As an Italian, I'm genuinely happy to see Italy so high in the ranking; our investments in national talent are paying off.

This seems to correlate with the acceptance rates in competitive funding schemes like the ERC grants. I'd be curious to know whether similar patterns also appear in other non-AI fields. I'm happy to hear thoughts from my @yacadeuro colleagues on this. Also, @EU_Commission, feel free to use the data. Personally, I find it great to see Poland growing so strongly; there is real talent there. Austria is also becoming increasingly visible, helped by new, forward-looking institutions. I'll rerun this analysis for ICML in a couple of months and am curious to see whether the same pattern holds.

When it comes to one-step discrete generation, almost everyone has been relying on flow maps. We took a different route: a normalizing flow. By learning the coupling between discrete sequences and noise directly, we avoid a high-dimensional flow.

The new approach proposes that instead of forcing discrete generation through a continuous flow map, we learn a coupling between discrete sequences and Gaussian noise. This allows us to train a one-step generator that directly predicts the sequence from the noise using cross-entropy. At inference time, generation is straightforward: sample Gaussian noise, decode once, and obtain a discrete sample without an autoregressive loop or iterative denoising trajectory. Empirically, Coupling Models improve strong one-step baselines across text generation, biological sequence generation, and binary image generation.

@pengzhangzhi1: "What is the right route to one-step discrete generation? Recently, the default answer has been: make discrete data continuous, learn a flow map, and then decode back to discrete samples. This is a natural route, but it is not the only one."

